We use the word ``anima'' for spaces/$\infty$-groupoids, and we denote by $\text{Ani}$ the $\infty$-category of anima. We write $\P(\mathcal{C})_\ast$ for the $\infty$-category of presheaves of pointed anima on $\mathcal{C}$. If $\tau$ is a Grothendieck topology on $\mathcal{C}$, we denote by $\P_{\tau}(\mathcal{C})_\ast \subseteq \P(\mathcal{C})_\ast$ the full subcategory of pointed $\tau$-sheaves. Following \cite{morel_homotopy_1999} (see \cite[Appendix~C]{hoyois_quadratic_2014} for a treatment without noetherian hypotheses), given a scheme $S$, a {\it pointed space} is an object of the category $\mathcal{P}_{\text{Nis}}(\text{Sm}_S)_\ast$, the category of {\it motivic spaces} $\text{H}(S)_\ast \subseteq \mathcal{P}_{\text{Nis}}(\text{Sm}_S)_\ast$ is the full subcategory of $\mathbb{A}^1$-invariant pointed spaces, and the category of {\it motivic spectra} $\text{SH}(S)$ is obtained by formally inverting $\mathbb{P}^1_S$ (seen as a motivic space pointed at infinity) in $\text{H}(S)_\ast$. All pointed spaces $E \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{Nis}}(\text{Sm}_S)_\ast$ are assumed to be finitary, and are implicitly extended from smooth to ind-smooth $S$-schemes by taking cofiltered limits, so that it is possible to evaluate $E$ on (henselian) local ind-smooth $S$-schemes.

