Concretely: in Section~\ref{sectionperverse} they define perverse $n$-connectivity and prove it is preserved by motivic localisation $L_{\text{mot}}$ (Corollary~\ref{corollaryLmotpreserveperverseconnectivity}); then in Section~\ref{sectionunstabletheorem} they use this to deduce the shifted unstable $\mathbb{A}^1$-connectivity theorem by passing from perverse to generic $(n-d)$-connectivity and then using a ``Gersten'' step to upgrade generic to actual $(n-d)$-connectivity.

